

SYSLOG(3)            UNIX Programmer's Manual             SYSLOG(3)



NAME
     syslog, openlog, closelog, setlogmask, vsyslog - control
     system log

SYNOPSIS
     #include <syslog.h>

     openlog(ident, logopt, facility)
     syslog(priority, message, ...)
     closelog()
     setlogmask(maskpri)

DESCRIPTION
     syslog() writes a message to the system log.  It forms the
     line

          <pri>Mon dd hh:mm:ss tag[pid]: message

     and sends it as one datagram to /dev/log, which is where a
     syslogd reads it from.  The bracketed pid appears only under
     LOG_PID.  The tag is what openlog() was given, or the
     program's own name if openlog() was not called.

     The message is a printf(3) format string, with one addition:
     %m is replaced by the error text for the errno current on
     entry, and consumes no argument.

     openlog() sets the tag, the option bits and the default
     facility.  LOG_PID prefixes the process id; LOG_CONS falls
     back to the console when the log cannot be reached;
     LOG_PERROR writes the line to standard error as well;
     LOG_NDELAY opens the log immediately rather than at the first
     syslog(); LOG_ODELAY is upstream's own no-op and LOG_NOWAIT
     is deprecated there.

     closelog() closes the descriptor.

     Priorities and facilities are the values in <syslog.h>, whose
     numbers match 2.11BSD's exactly so that a ported source
     compares against the same constants upstream did.

     WHERE THE LINE GOES WHEN NOTHING IS LISTENING.  FGX has no
     syslogd yet.  With nothing bound to /dev/log the datagram is
     dropped, which is what 2.11BSD does when syslogd is not
     running.  Two fallbacks remain, and both are upstream's own:
     when the socket cannot be created at all -- a boot with no
     net module, so there is no transport -- the line is appended
     to /usr/adm/messages; and a caller that asked for LOG_CONS
     gets it on /dev/console.

SEE ALSO
     unix(4), printf(3), strerror(3)

BUGS
     setlogmask() NEVER FILTERS ANYTHING.  It records the mask and
     returns the previous one, and no priority is ever suppressed.
     This is upstream's defect, carried deliberately: the test in
     2.11BSD's vsyslog reads

          if (!LOG_MASK(LOG_PRI(pri)) & LogMask)

     where the unary ! binds tighter than the &, so its left
     operand is the negation of LOG_MASK alone -- which is 1 << pri
     for a priority in 0 through 7, never zero, so the negation is
     zero and the test is never true.  NetBSD 0.9 carries the same
     expression and 4.3BSD-Reno a different equally non-filtering
     one.  Diverging would make this drop messages that 2.11BSD
     delivers, so a program calling setlogmask() behaves exactly
     as it does upstream: it is heard in full.

     THE LOG DESCRIPTOR SURVIVES exec(2).  Upstream marks it
     close-on-exec; there is no fcntl(2) here and no
     close-on-exec bit in the kernel, so a process that logs and
     then execs passes the descriptor to its child.

     THE TIMESTAMP IS BUILT FROM ctime(3) rather than
     strftime(3).  The bytes are identical -- 4.3BSD-Reno and
     NetBSD 0.9 form the line this way and 2.11BSD uses strftime
     -- but a program that expects its own strftime settings to
     reach the log line will not find them doing so.

     With no syslogd(8) running, a line sent to a /dev/log nobody
     has bound goes nowhere and says so to no one, which is what
     2.11BSD does in the same state.

HISTORY
     These functions appeared in 4.2BSD.
